        (1043, null, '[]', 'Use rules, skills, and hooks to modify and extend Cursor.  

Hi Nguyen Pham Thu Ha,

Coding agents can learn how you like to write software, your preferred tools, and the context of your business.

Use these three features to modify and extend agent behavior:

Rules

Define rules ( https://cursor.com/docs/context/rules ) to teach Cursor your best practices, from team conventions to specific architectural decisions.

Define rules in Cursor

Try creating a rule ( https://cursor.com/link/rule?name=example-rule&text=Prefer%20clear%2C%20self-documenting%20code.%20Add%20comments%20only%20for%20non-obvious%20%22why%22%20decisions.%20Use%20meaningful%20names%20for%20variables%20and%20functions.%20Keep%20functions%20small%20and%20focused. )Skills

Create skills ( https://cursor.com/docs/context/skills ) to let Cursor discover and run specialized prompts and code with domain-specific knowledge.

Create skills in Cursor

Hooks

Set up hooks ( https://cursor.com/docs/agent/hooks ) for deterministic scripts that observe and control agent behavior at defined stages of the loop.

Set up hooks in Cursor

You can discover and install prebuilt plugins containing rules, skills, hooks, MCP, and more on the Cursor Marketplace ( https://cursor.com/marketplace ).

Explore plugins ( https://cursor.com/marketplace )Best,
