        (1060, null, '[]', 'Hello Ha,  


You''ve prompted an agent to "create me a button". It''s almost right. So you correct it. Then again. And again. By the end, you have a long, messy dialogue – and code you can''t actually maintain.

That''s vibe coding. It works for buttons. It collapses on real projects.

Our new short course, Spec-Driven Development With Coding Agents, teaches you the professional alternative. You''ll build AgentClinic – a full-stack TypeScript app – from constitution to MVP, watching every architectural decision land in code.

Enroll free at DeepLearning.AI https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/spec-driven-development-with-coding-agents/?map=2

You''ll learn how to:

- &#x2705; Write specs that preserve context across agent sessions.
- &#x2705; Run the plan → implement → validate loop on every feature, with the human in the loop where it matters.
- &#x2705; Automate your workflow with Skills, MCP, and the emerging agent standards (AGENTS​.​md, ACP).
- &#x2705; Stay agent-agnostic – switch between Claude Code, Codex, or OpenCode without rebuilding your workflow.


Why now? Coding agents have outpaced the workflows we use to control them. Specs persist where context windows decay. They''re how senior engineers stay senior in the agentic era.

<p><br /></p><p>Keep learning,<br /><strong>The JetBrains Academy team</strong></p><p>Make it happen. With code.</p>
